WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.202 --> 00:00:02.660
RETO MEIER: Today I learned
that when it snows in New York,

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:04.640
manhole covers go missing.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.140
Summertime means long city bike
rides, dead trees, and rats.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.720
And, well, something
happens with dogs in July.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:13.490
I'm Reto Meier, Google
developer advocate.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.960
Join me as I use the call
function, 30 gigabytes

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.580
of weather records, and 130 gigs
of New York City public data

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:22.520
to investigate correlations,
if not causations,

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.680
with weather in New York
City, with BigQuery.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.299
Trapped in the unchanging,
timeless stasis

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:35.840
of this top secret and totally
real Google data center,

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.420
I don't get much in
the way of weather.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.220
But that doesn't mean
I can't use BigQuery

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.345
to see how the weather
affects the lucky people who

00:00:42.345 --> 00:00:46.340
get to experience Outer World.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.920
For example, as the
temperature rises into the 70s,

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.680
people take more city bike
trips, and more of those rides

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.450
start from the outer boroughs.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:55.190
That's not really surprising.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.060
Where it gets interesting is
the unexpected correlations,

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.370
like snow and missing
manhole covers.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:03.140
To find more weather
correlations,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.600
we need to dig through
200 gigabytes of data.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.550
BigQuery is fast
and cheap, but it'll

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:11.600
be faster and cheaper if we make
our data a bit more manageable.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.210
The NOAA global
summary of day tables

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:15.920
include a dozen
weather variables

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.750
recorded from 9,000 stations
dating back to 1929.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.450
And we don't need all of that.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.310
We'll extract just the weather
variables we need and replace

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:28.820
the 999.99 values, which
are used to represent null,

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:32.120
with zeros, so that our averages
make a little bit more sense.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.190
Airports tend to provide
the best weather data.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:36.530
And in New York, that
means LaGuardia and JFK.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:40.270
So we'll select just those and
average their daily records.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.990
The NOAA tables
are split by year,

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:45.020
so let's combine everything
from 2009 to 2016,

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.280
the date range for our
New York data sets.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.870
Saving the result
to its own table

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:54.920
results in a very
manageable 200 kilobytes.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.400
Now, using our
weather table, let's

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.850
see if temperatures have
any effect on cab rides.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.830
Unlike the city
bikes, this graph

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:04.400
doesn't show a strong pattern.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.870
But what other interesting
patterns can we find?

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.550
We all know that correlation
doesn't imply causation.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.600
But maybe, if we're just looking
for interesting patterns,

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.270
we can use correlations
as a way of finding them.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.840
Calculating correlation
is a pain in the [DING].

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.330
It's hard, and graphing every
possibility would take forever.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:24.700
But thankfully,
BigQuery includes

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.790
the call function to do
all the hard work for us.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:29.830
This query returns
the correlations

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:31.480
between each of our
weather variables

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:34.750
and each of the primary causes
of motor vehicle accidents.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.600
The results table
suggests two possibility--

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.540
accidents caused by slippery
pavements related to snow,

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.630
and wind gusts related
to runaway cars.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.030
Graphing both against
time, there's some evidence

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:47.560
that slipping
accidents increases

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.210
when there's heavy snow.

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.550
What about gusty winds
pushing runaway cars

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.410
down the streets of New York?

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:57.260
I'd like it to be true, but
does the data support it?

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.680
Well, let's say for now
it's too early to tell.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.840
We've only got data for runaway
cars for the last four months.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.010
And when we graph it, there's
just not enough there.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:07.480
So where to next?

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.760
BigQuery includes every
municipal complaint

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.000
made to 311, so there's dozens
of different complaint types

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.280
that we can try and link
to weather patterns.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.110
Using the call
function, I ran queries

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:20.350
to find the top correlations for
temperature, snow depth, wind

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.540
speed, wind gusts, and rain.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.430
The most likely candidates?

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:27.100
Temperature relationships with
heating complaints, dead trees,

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.470
and rat sightings, snow
depth relationships

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:31.600
with missing manhole
covers and illegal parking,

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.350
and higher wind speeds with
lower noise complaints.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:39.131
You can find a link to the full
table and the queries here.

00:03:39.131 --> 00:03:41.380
Now, it's no surprise that
the call volume for heating

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.030
complaints is
significantly higher

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.740
when the temperature is lower.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:47.380
What is surprising was
a similar correlation

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:50.110
between missing manhole
covers and snow depth.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:52.720
So either the Abominable
Snowman is a New Yorker

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.460
with a penchant
for manhole covers,

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.407
or, if we have our
causation reversed,

00:03:57.407 --> 00:03:59.740
manhole covers are what's
preventing New York from being

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.771
swallowed by a snowpocalypse.

00:04:01.771 --> 00:04:04.270
Now, whether it's a snowman or
a snowpocalypse, when graphed

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:06.880
over time, there is a
fairly consistent pattern

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.450
of heavy snow and reports
for missing manhole covers.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.150
The amount of snow doesn't
seem that significant.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.160
Maybe the holes in the
road are more noticeable

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.529
when the rest of
the street is white.

00:04:17.529 --> 00:04:21.399
Snow depth and illegal parking
turns out to be a dead end.

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:24.220
But hydrant complaints are
trending up, as is snowfall.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.530
And while double parking
had some coincident spikes

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:29.350
with heavy snow
in 2010 and 2016,

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:31.300
there wasn't really
much else there.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:34.180
Either way, New Yorkers,
maybe stop parking

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:35.590
in front of fire hydrants?

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:36.645
Not cool.

00:04:36.645 --> 00:04:38.020
You noticed that
there were a lot

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.478
of strong correlations with
temperature, such as complaints

00:04:40.478 --> 00:04:41.290
about trees.

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:42.790
And the clearest
thing in this graph

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:45.292
is that there's a seasonal
pattern affecting both,

00:04:45.292 --> 00:04:46.750
which is made even
clearer when you

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:48.610
look at the monthly averages.

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:50.260
So let's take those
monthly averages

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:51.880
and seasonally
adjust our results

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:54.910
to see if there's a pattern
beyond the time of year.

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:56.380
This graph measures
the proportion

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:58.150
of the month's
tree-related calls

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:00.730
to temperature minus
the monthly average.

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:04.050
And there's really not much
to speak of in terms of trend.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:06.070
Now, wind speed had
a strong correlation

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:07.270
with noise complaints.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.030
We know New York City has
windier weather in the winter,

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.800
so let's just seasonally
adjust for that right away.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.250
If we graph all our
points as a scatterplot,

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:17.650
you can almost see
a relationship.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:19.810
But there's also a lot of noise.

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:22.810
And here we've put our results
into buckets of one degree,

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:24.719
averaging the volumes
for each bucket.

00:05:24.719 --> 00:05:26.260
And the relationship
is much clearer,

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.570
but it's also a
little bit deceptive.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.132
It totally hides the variance
and gives undue weight

00:05:31.132 --> 00:05:32.590
to the buckets at
the extremes that

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:35.140
represent far fewer
samples, which

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:38.390
is why we have the magic
of the hex spin graph.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:40.945
Here we can graph all the
points within a given hex

00:05:40.945 --> 00:05:42.650
and weight the
darkness of each hex

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:44.830
by the number of
points it contains.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:47.350
As a result, we can see a
trend from the overall shape

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:50.170
of the distribution, as well
as a heat map of measurement

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:51.880
distribution.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:54.410
For wind and noise, you can
see a triangular pattern

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.500
that implies a weak
negative correlation, which

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:59.140
suggests a greater likelihood
of getting more noise

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:01.620
complaints when the wind is
low and significantly fewer

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.340
calls when the
wind is five knots

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:05.170
above the monthly average--

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:07.930
particularly for loud
talking, which almost

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:10.540
gives us a band in a
diagonal line for both

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:14.109
the overall distribution and the
area of highest concentration.

00:06:14.109 --> 00:06:14.650
[DOG BARKING]

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:18.070
Neither approach explains
barking dogs, though.

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:19.310
It's troubling.

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:22.810
Why do barking dog
complaints go down in summer?

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:24.420
What happens to the dogs?

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:25.487
Are they on vacation?

00:06:25.487 --> 00:06:27.070
Do they share a house
in the Hamptons?

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:28.030
Are they locked outside?

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:29.020
Are they locked inside?

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:30.017
Why are they silent?

00:06:30.017 --> 00:06:32.350
I think we've stumbled on
something here, something big.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:33.724
We've got to get
the message out.

00:06:33.724 --> 00:06:34.645
There's no telling--

00:06:34.645 --> 00:06:36.070
[BEEP]

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:37.930
Check out the link below
to find the largest

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:40.740
subset of possible weather
complaints correlations.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:44.580
And please, dig in to find other
nuggets hidden within the data.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:47.080
As always, with Big Query, your
first terabyte of processing

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:48.540
each month is free.

